WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.500 --> 00:00:05.000
Lesson 9.7 · Financial Management
Management accounts and budget versus actual

2
00:00:05.600 --> 00:00:11.800
Compare actual with budget every month to see quickly which first-year assumptions were wrong.

3
00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:26.281
Management accounts are short internal reports, monthly or quarterly, that compare actual income and spending with your budget. They exist to help you decide, so on time and simple beats perfect.

4
00:00:26.681 --> 00:00:27.281
From numbers to decisions

5
00:00:27.281 --> 00:00:38.981
Budget versus actual: For each line, the budget to date, the actual to date and the difference (the variance). For example: Ingredients budget US$1,200, actual US$1,450: a variance of US$250 over budget.

6
00:00:38.981 --> 00:00:49.570
Variance explained: One sentence for each big difference: timing, price or volume. For example: Volume: the stall sold 360 loaves a week, not 300, because demand was higher.

7
00:00:49.570 --> 00:00:58.678
Decision: Accept the variance, correct it or change the forecast. For example: The founders accept the extra baking and raise the ingredients forecast.

8
00:00:58.678 --> 00:01:11.207
Year-end forecast: Where you will end up, and whether that is acceptable. For example: At this rate the year ends US$400 short, so the founders delay buying a second mixer.

9
00:01:11.607 --> 00:01:12.207
Why the monthly check matters

10
00:01:12.207 --> 00:01:18.252
1. Your first budget is mostly assumptions; this shows quickly which were wrong.

11
00:01:18.252 --> 00:01:24.296
2. It turns numbers into decisions while there is still time to act.

12
00:01:24.296 --> 00:01:30.970
3. The routine builds the habit funders and a future board expect.

13
00:01:31.370 --> 00:01:31.970
What they feed

14
00:01:31.970 --> 00:01:36.533
1. Your monthly finance check, even between two co-founders.

15
00:01:36.533 --> 00:01:41.096
2. Your cash flow forecast and next year’s budget.

16
00:01:41.096 --> 00:01:47.030
3. Your reports to funders and, later, to a board.

17
00:01:47.430 --> 00:01:48.030
What to avoid

18
00:01:48.030 --> 00:01:54.074
Waiting for perfect figures, so the report arrives too late to help.

19
00:01:54.074 --> 00:01:58.637
Listing variances without explaining them or deciding anything.

20
00:01:58.637 --> 00:02:06.052
Asking only “where are we now?” and never “where will we end up?”

21
00:02:06.652 --> 00:02:08.452
You design your first report and the review routine you start with.

22
00:02:08.452 --> 00:02:12.452
Then save it, and download it as a PDF.

23
00:02:13.952 --> 00:02:17.052
Method into Impact
Better foundations. Stronger organisations.
